    qs: [
      { q: `Why is the median often reported instead of the mean for something like household income?`, ch: [`The median is mathematically always the larger of the two given values`, `Reporting a mean for income data is technically against the law`, `The mean and median always end up producing identical values`, `The median isn't distorted by extreme outliers the way the mean can be`], ans: 3, exp: `A small number of extremely high incomes can pull the mean upward significantly, while the median (the middle value) stays representative of a "typical" household.` },
      { q: `What does a probability of 0 versus 1 represent?`, ch: [`0 means the event is impossible, 1 means it's certain`, `0 means the event is certain, 1 means it's impossible`, `Both values represent an identical likelihood of occurring`, `Probabilities are actually never expressed using this scale`], ans: 0, exp: `Probability is scored from 0 (the event cannot happen) to 1 (the event is certain to happen), with everything else falling somewhere in between.` },
      { q: `What does "statistically significant" actually mean?`, ch: [`That the observed effect is large and clinically important`, `That the result is unlikely to have occurred by random chance alone`, `That the underlying study contained absolutely no design flaws whatsoever at all`, `That the treatment definitely works for every single patient`], ans: 1, exp: `Statistical significance only speaks to how unlikely a result is to be due to chance — it says nothing on its own about how big or clinically meaningful the effect is.` },
      { q: `Why can a very large sample size produce a "statistically significant" result that's still practically unimportant?`, ch: [`Because large samples reliably tend to produce false results`, `Because sample size has essentially no real effect on statistical significance`, `Enough data can make even a tiny, real difference statistically detectable`, `Because this particular situation basically never actually happens`], ans: 2, exp: `Statistical power increases with sample size, so very large studies can detect even minuscule effects as "significant" even when the real-world impact is negligible.` },
      { q: `Ice cream sales and drowning rates rise together in the summer. What does this best illustrate?`, ch: [`That eating ice cream directly and reliably causes drowning`, `That drowning incidents somehow directly cause a noticeable rise in ice cream sales`, `Two unrelated variables that coincide with no real explanation`, `Correlation without causation, where a third factor like heat drives both`], ans: 3, exp: `Both variables rise because of a shared underlying cause (hot weather, more swimming, more ice cream) — not because one causes the other.` },
    ],
